/*
 * `_restrict` formulas over AC-headed terms, one wholly free and one mixing a
 * bound variable with free ones.
 *
 * `rewrite`'s third guard substitutes a whole application whose arguments
 * carry free variables and no bound one (Theory/Model/Restriction.hs:103-105),
 * so `(y add x) ++ x` becomes a single fresh `x` and the rule's action carries
 * the term itself — in the AC-normal argument order `fApp` gave it
 * (Term/Term/Raw.hs:111-127), not the written one.
 *
 * The fourth guard rebuilds an application that mixes the two through
 * `termViewToTerm` (Theory/Model/Restriction.hs:106-110), the RAW constructor
 * (Term/Term/Raw.hs:103-105), which applies no AC normalisation: the rebuilt
 * `++` keeps the operand positions the traversal saw, and the fresh variables
 * are numbered in that same order.
 *
 * `add` is a user `[AC]` symbol, which `prettyTerm` renders with spaces around
 * the operator (Term/Term.hs:305) while `++` prints without them
 * (Term/Term.hs:308), so the echo tells the two chains apart.
 *
 * No corpus theory reaches this: no `_restrict` in the examples tree mentions
 * a multiset or a user-AC symbol.
 *
 * Mode `match`: the two engines agree on this shape.
 *
 * Compared slice: the `theory … end` echo (see check.sh).
 */
theory S6RestrictFreeAc
begin

builtins: multiset
functions: add/2 [AC]

rule A:
  [ In( x ), In( y ) ]
  --[ _restrict( Ex #i. Ev( (y add x) ++ x ) @ #i ),
      _restrict( Ex z #i. Ev( (z add x) ++ y ) @ #i ) ]->
  [ ]

end
